            <div class="modal-body">
              <p>Surekha Yadav became India and Asia&#8217;s first female locopilot (train driver) for the Indian Railways in 1988. In April 2000, when it was initially established in the four major metro cities the then-railway minister, she was the first &#8220;Ladies Special&#8221; local train driver for Central Railways. She made history on March 8, 2011, International Women&#8217;s Day, when she became Asia&#8217;s first woman train driver to operate the Deccan Queen from Pune to CST Mumbai through challenging but beautiful terrain. At CST, the headquarters of the Central Railway zone, she was met by the then-Mayor of Mumbai, Shraddha Jadhav.<br><br> Surekha&#8217;s dream came true when she took the wheel of one of the Central Railway trains carrying the named female; she had received significant backing from the Mumbai-Pune Railway Pravasi Sangh (organisation) to operate this train. Ten years later, she carried out the same feat while driving an all-female crew from Mumbai to Lucknow. In 2011, the statement &#8220;Women don&#8217;t run railroad engines&#8221; was often repeated.</p>
