            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">
              <p>Gulabo Sapera, a famous creator of the dynamic and graceful Kalbelia dance motions that resemble the movements of a serpent, was born in the nomadic Kalbeliya community in 1973. She also came up with a unique song and Kalbelia-specific black ghagra-choli and dupatta with multicoloured piping. She has worked with French musicians to create several French-language music CDs, and a book and documentary have both been published on her. She has a street named after her in France. In the US, Canada, Japan, Chile, and France, Gulabo Sapera has a sizable fan base. She received the Padma Shri in 2016 in appreciation of her efforts to promote the Kalbelia folk dance internationally.<br><br> Due to Gulabo, the Kalbelia dance, which had previously received no acknowledgment, was included to UNESCO&#8217;s representative list of Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2010. Gulabo Sapera has also starred in a number of Bollywood and Rajasthani movies, as well as the reality shows. Gulabo, a self-made lady, is an inspiration to all of us. She never had the chance to go to school, therefore she is now working to make her Kalbeliya community stronger and ensuring that their girls may receive a formal education. According to Gulabo, she wishes to leave her heritage to her pupils so that they can carry on the Kalbelia dance tradition. She runs a school in Pushkar, close to Ajmer, and one in Denmark.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
