              <p>Always creative, Rashi Kankaria chanced upon this field when her son was born and she started photographing him. Today, 120 newborn shoots later, Rashi prides herself on the fact that every single of those families has gone away satisfied. Certified by the Guild of Photographers, UK, Rashi&#8217;s work spans nearly a decade, in which she has carved out a niche for herself in newborn children&#8217;s photography. According to her, practise, skill and patience are the three essentials for photographing babies. Looking ahead, she wants to achieve a distinct sense of style so that her work is recognizable to everyone and plans to continue expanding to other cities. </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="vidishaParasramka" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Vidisha Parasramka</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>The moment Vidisha Parasramka picked up the camera and took her first few pictures, she knew she had found her calling. She wanted to carve out a reputation for herself in child and family photography; to provide memories to a new mother that she would cherish forever. Her clients swear by her, claiming she has “the patience to handle the naughtiest child ever” and that her “passion for photography is visible in every picture”. Vidisha’s role model was her grandfather, who motivated her to excel in whatever she took up, and following his advice she aspires to get even bigger and better at what she does.
</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="kasturiMukherjee" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Kasturi Mukherjee</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Kasturi Mukherjee grew up in a household of creative heads. Surrounded by photography enthusiasts, she was naturally drawn towards it as well. Later on, inspired by Homi Vyarawalla—India’s first female photojournalist—Kasturi went on to get a degree in Journalism &#038; Mass Communication. Not yet 30, she already has a number of achievements under her belt, from winning awards and being published in various magazines such as Better Photography and Vogue Italy to being a team photographer for the Kolkata Knight Riders (2015). A longtime dream of hers is to work towards the betterment and acceptance of the LGBTQ+ community at the grassroots level in West Bengal.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="anjaliKanakia" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Anjali Kanakia</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>A passion-turned-profession is how Anjali Kanakia describes her relationship of over 10 years with photography. To her it was something that came right from the heart; looking at an object differently, from a new angle, and finding beauty in the simplest of things. Despite not having any mentoring or guidance when she started out, her persistence and hard work paid off; her decade of work includes brands and celebrities such as Taj Group of Hotels, Nawazuddin Siddiqui, Shyamika &#038; Bhumal and Esha Thirani for Paris Fashion Week. When it comes to mentoring upcoming talent, Anjali chooses to preach what she practices. For herself, she wants to keep growing, stay current, inspire and be inspired. </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="upaharBiswas" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Upahar Biswas</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Upahar Biswas stumbled upon her passion through her mother’s Kodak KB10 when she was just 7. She began her career as a photojournalist. Now, 5 years into her journey, she has shot multiple national campaigns, hosted workshops, judged contests in educational institutions and even hosted a group exhibition in photography. Upahar identifies with people and brands that are “fun, bold, and experimental”. A seeker of beauty, she aims to bring novelty into this field; she strives to create fresh concepts and make her art commercially available. She asserts the importance of staying grounded and focused in this line of work and hopes to go on to shoot with international brands.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="sohiniChakraborty" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Dr Sohini Chakraborty</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Dr Sohini Chakraborty, an Ashoka fellow, sociologist, dance activist and a dance movement therapist, is the founder of the NGO, Kolkata Sanved. A pioneer of Dance Movement Therapy (DMT) in India and South Asia, she launched DMT as a tool for psycho-social rehabilitation to provide a fresh approach in the social development sector. She is a part of the International Working Committee Members of the Rise Learning Network-Working for recovery and reintegration for child sexual exploitation and Chair-Status &#038; Development at World Dance Alliance, Asia Pacific.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="urmiBasu" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Ms Urmi Basu</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Ms Urmi Basu is the founder of New Light, a secular NGO that operates as a creche-cum-night shelter to protect and educate young girls, children and women at high risk in a red light area of Kolkata. Urmi felt the need to do something where she could see her work impact the lives of people, turning oppression into opportunity for women worldwide. She has been an awardee of the Nari Shakti Puraskar, meaning ‘Woman Power Award’, India&#8217;s highest civilian award that recognizes the achievements and contributions of women. According to her, patience, perseverance and resilience are the tools required for any long-term social action initiative.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="payalNath" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Ms Payal Nath</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Ms Payal Nath believes happiness given is happiness multiplied, and that’s what drives her every day. She started Kadam Haat, a platform to support the products that women make. This venture is dedicated to skilling and uplifting women in rural India, an effort to sow the seed of entrepreneurship in these women. Something that always intrigued Payal was her mother’s sense of detachment, inspired by the philosophy in Bhagavad Gita: work without expecting a reward. She instilled adaptability and an insatiable hunger for challenges. One such hunger was to create change, which inevitably led to the launch of the Kadam Foundation. Payal was recipient of the Social Entrepreneur of the Year award (2019) by Amazon.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="srabaniSarkar" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Ms Srabani Sarkar Neogi</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Ms Srabani Sarkar Neogi is Founder Secretary and Director of South Kolkata Hamari Muskan (SKHM), an anti-trafficking organization. Srabani is a social development activist with 20 years of experience working in red light areas (RLAs) with women in prostitution (WIP) and (their) children. Her goal is to use educational and psychological interventions to create an ultimate resilient being: one who allows no torture, no injustice against self or society; one who can unite others to start a movement and create an egalitarian world.  Srabani was awarded the Karmaveer Puraskaar (2015), ICONGO National People’s Award for Social Justice and Action.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="geetaVenkadakrishnan" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Geeta Venkadakrishnan</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Ms Geeta Venkadakrishnan is the Director of the Hope Kolkata Foundation, an organisation founded for the safety and growth of women and children on the streets of Kolkata. She is the recipient of awards including the Good Samaritan Award from Kolkata Police, Bharat Yogya Ratna Award, Rashtriya Pragati Award and others. Ms Venkadakrishnan has partaken in relief work, rescuing and rebuilding the lives of victims of the Bhuj earthquake, Kolkata floods and the tsunami. Keeping Mother Teresa as a role model, Geeta hopes to ensure shelter and safety for street children and vulnerable women in Kolkata, creating a world where it never hurts to be a child.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="premlataAshs" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Premlata Bhuwalka, Asha Saboo, Late Sudha Khemkha, Sneh Periwal &#038; Brij Jhawar</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Jagriti is a global non-profit organisation that has effectively conducted a number of projects to lift the helpless and underprivileged. The journey started in 1960 with a small group of members &#8211; Sudha Khemka, Brij Jhawar, Asha Saboo, Sneh Periwal and Premlata Bhuwalka, who had a common dream of social upliftment. Imparting education, rehabilitation of the needy and lending a helping hand to the victims of natural and man-made calamities are some of the past projects that Jagriti has conducted. They also generate awareness on important social issues amongst people from all strata of society.
</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="bratatiBhattacharyya" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Bratati Bhattacharyya</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>A gold medallist from Jadavpur University, Bratati Bhattacharyya was an Investment Banker for seven years before being drawn to the world of Education. Starting off as a high school teacher, she went on to become the Vice Principal of Ashok Hall and then the Principal of GD Birla Centre for Education. Currently, she is the <b>Secretary General and CEO of Shikshayatan Foundation</b> which manages Marwari Balika Vidyalaya, Shri Shikshayatan School, Shri Shikshayatan College and Calcutta Business School. A recipient of noteworthy awards such as ‘Indian Iconic Educationist’ ‘Inspiring Best Teacher’ and ‘Bharat Vidya Shiromani’ Bratati calls herself a lifelong learner. She is the Chief Education Officer of Technology group of School and promoter of a pre primary school.Currently, she is also pursuing her Doctoral studies from University of Liverpool. Her future plans include a skill development centre and introducing sports management courses.
</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="kavneetKhullar" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Kavneet Khullar</h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Kavneet Khullar started her career at the Indian Institute of Cerebral Palsy and then joined Ms Neena Singh, to make it the first Inclusive Montessori. Recognising the need for inclusion of children with ‘Additional Needs’ in a mainstream school, Akshar the first Inclusive I.S.C School in Kolkata was established in 1998. Kavneet who believes that it is the right of children with disabilities to study alongside mainstream children, is the Trustee and the Principal of Akshar. Living up to the maxim, “The world would alas be so much duller, if we were all one colour,” she is a member of the Internal Quality Assurance Cell (IQAC), Loreto College in the field of Education and the Trustee and Founder of The Harrington Street Arts Centre. Her labour of love of 33 years, Akshar has been the recipient of numerous prestigious awards, including The Telegraph School Awards, for The School of the Year, 2018. Being environmentally conscious, she would like to make Akshar an environmentally conscious and sustainable hub with an aim to minimise waste.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="sharmisthaChatterjeey" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="modal-dialog  modal-dialog-centered modal-lg" role="document">
        <div class="modal-content">
            <div class="modal-header">                
                <h5 class="modal-title" id="exampleModalLabel">Dr. Sharmistha Chatterjee </h5>
               <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary">X</button>
            </div>
            <div class="modal-body">              
              <p>Dr. Sharmistha Chatterjee, an archaeologist by profession, has obtained her PhD and Gold medal from the Department of Archaeology, University of Calcutta. Her Post Doctoral research is from the Indian Council of Historical Research (ICHR, New Delhi). She has been awarded international Fellowships like CWIT and Nehru Trust to work in the British Museum, Victoria Albert Museum, London and Ashmolean in Oxford. Her research has covered entire stretches of the riverine valleys of the western uplands of Bengal in the districts of Bankura, Bardhaman and Medinipur. She has discovered 64 archaeological sites in the vicinity of the medieval temple town of Bishnupur (Bankura, West Bengal). Making a mark in the traditionally patriarchal field of archaeology has come with its own challenges. Whilst pursuing her PhD, Sharmishtha was stuck in dense forests and remote villages without food or communication. Working for women, particularly in the rural areas is her passion and she has published extensive papers on gender studies in order to facilitate the betterment of these women. She was also a Visiting Research Fellow in the University of Belfast.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<div class="modal fade" id="neerjaRateria" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="exampleModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
